WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.276
Open your project in KiCad 10 and go to the PCB editor.

2
00:00:04.276 --> 00:00:06.747
The KiCad IA button is in the toolbar.

3
00:00:06.747 --> 00:00:10.388
Pressing it starts the plugin's server on your computer.

4
00:00:11.988 --> 00:00:15.186
The chat opens in your browser, at a local address.

5
00:00:15.186 --> 00:00:19.513
The top bar shows whether KiCad is responding, which model is active,

6
00:00:19.513 --> 00:00:21.645
and how many tokens you have used.

7
00:00:21.645 --> 00:00:24.655
On the left, the open project and its libraries.

8
00:00:26.255 --> 00:00:28.632
Describe the circuit in your own words.

9
00:00:28.632 --> 00:00:33.021
For example: an LED with its resistor, powered at five volts over USB-C.

10
00:00:33.021 --> 00:00:38.507
The chat first pins down what you asked for and looks for each part in your own libraries.

11
00:00:38.507 --> 00:00:41.007
Every tool shows on screen while it runs.

12
00:00:42.607 --> 00:00:46.095
When something is missing, it asks instead of guessing.

13
00:00:46.095 --> 00:00:51.422
Here the resistor and the LED have no footprint, so it offers the packages it found.

14
00:00:51.422 --> 00:00:55.101
It also proposes two 5.1 kilohm resistors on the CC lines,

15
00:00:55.101 --> 00:00:56.749
and waits for your answer.

16
00:00:58.349 --> 00:01:03.021
With your answer it writes the schematic, keeps a backup, and checks it with ERC.

17
00:01:03.021 --> 00:01:05.674
To write, the schematic editor must be closed.

18
00:01:05.674 --> 00:01:08.615
Then it tells you the next step and the board size.

19
00:01:10.215 --> 00:01:15.281
In the PCB editor, open Tools and choose Update PCB from Schematic, or press F8.

20
00:01:15.281 --> 00:01:19.713
This step is yours: the plugin does not import the netlist on its own.

21
00:01:19.713 --> 00:01:23.006
The footprints arrive together, outside the outline.

22
00:01:24.606 --> 00:01:25.679
Ask for placement.

23
00:01:25.679 --> 00:01:31.701
The plugin computes a proposal with an IPC Class 2 pre-check and shows it without touching the board.

24
00:01:31.701 --> 00:01:34.682
If you set up a reviewer model, it reviews it too.

25
00:01:34.682 --> 00:01:36.828
It is only applied when you confirm.

26
00:01:38.428 --> 00:01:41.450
With the footprints in place, ask for autorouting.

27
00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:43.927
FreeRouting works on a copy of the board.

28
00:01:43.927 --> 00:01:47.976
The plugin runs DRC and shows you the candidate before applying it.

29
00:01:47.976 --> 00:01:50.876
On this board: zero errors and no unrouted nets.

30
00:01:52.476 --> 00:01:56.340
You confirm, and the copper goes to the board open in KiCad.

31
00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.110
If you like, ask for a 3D view to check it.

32
00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:03.680
Back in KiCad, the board is placed and routed.

33
00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:07.877
Check it, save it, and carry on with your usual fabrication flow.
